  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday...\n\nSatellite imagery shows a flat ridge axis over the high plains, a\nlow with cold air aloft off the Pacific coast, and a moistening SW\nflow from CA to western MT. At the surface, downslope gradients\nremain and wind gusts are still elevated at Livingston & Nye, but\nthey have weakened since Sunday as the backing flow aloft reduces\nthe gradient. Expect lighter winds thru the day with temps in the\n50s to lower 60s, warmest east. Heights will fall steadily on \nTuesday as a trof moves inland. Tomorrow will be the last warm day\nof the week with highs ranging from the upper 40s northwest to \nlower 60s southeast. \n\nChanges begin late Tuesday as a wave kicks northeastward thru the\ngreat basin and brings us a good chance of precip Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday. A few things to note. Today's models have come \ninto better agreement w/ upper level forcing and have trended \nwetter. There remain differences with regard to where a surface \nlow develops early Wednesday (in eastern MT or near the MT/WY \nline), and this will impact precip amounts and the transition to \ngusty downslope winds, and also whether or not there are potential\npre-frontal mountain waves in the lee of the Bighorns. Either way,\nit will become windy on Wednesday w/ post-frontal W-NW winds \nlikely to gust 30-45 mph, highest in the east and on the east side\nof the Bighorns. These areas have a 50-70% chance of gusts over \n40 mph. Something to monitor. Ensembles show precip amounts of \n0.10-0.35\" west of Rosebud County, a little more over the western \nmountains and less in the southeast. Again, this may change for \nthe reasons already mentioned. Regarding snowfall, this looks like\na 3-6\" event for the western mountains, and there is a 40% chance\nof exceeding 6\" on favored W-NW aspects of the Absarokas south of\nLivingston. Lower elevation precip will remain mostly rain, but \nif the mid level low deepens a bit there could be enough dynamic \ncooling for a mix or change to wet snow over the higher hills east\nof Billings. Places like Arrow Creek Hill, Wolf Mountains, Hysham\nHills and Lame Deer Divide could see light accumulations. Look \nfor high temps in the low to mid 40s on Wednesday, which with the \nwind will feel quite chilly compared to what we've experienced so \nfar in November.\n\nPrecip will taper off from west to east Wednesday, ending in the\nfar east by evening. Brief period of ridging should bring dry wx\nWednesday night & Thursday before the next system arrives. Look\nfor temps in the upper 30s to mid 40s on Thursday.\n\nThere is potential for a cooler and wetter weather system to end\nthe week but must continue to stress high uncertainty in details.\nPacific low is expected to evolve into an open wave as it moves \nthrough the northern and central Rockies in some fashion Thursday \nnight into Saturday. Confidence is high for cooler temps (dare we\nsay cooler than normal?) with a 40-60% chance of precip, and snow \nlevels supportive of lower elevation snow during this period.\nConfidence is low for upslope potential and the location of\nheaviest precip. Upslope flow would yield heavier precip near the\nfoothills but it is also possible that downslope winds keep best \nchances for the mountains and east. It is also possible that a \nsurface low develops over the plains to our east, bringing snow \nand gusty winds to southeast MT on the back side of the trof. All \nquestions that do not have answers at this time, so please monitor\nthe forecast over the coming days. Areas east of the foothills \nmay see their first snow of the season, and wintry impacts to \ntravel are possible.\n\nEnsemble consensus is for drying by Saturday but another Pacific\nwave may arrive by Sunday or Monday. Look for near to slightly\ncooler than normal temps for the weekend. JKL",
